    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.63, 390.522, 286.1, 449.917 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Looking at (1) gives us the needed electrical parameters to be defined. We find R and L, the resistance and the inductance of the coil, µf, the force constant, and µbemf, the bemf (back electromagnetic force) constant of the system."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.651, 342.256, 285.98, 389.55 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "R and L are the intrinsic electrical properties of the coil and are purely passive. A simple measurement on the RLC meter will allow us to get those values. These are given in Table 3."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.641, 318.024, 285.98, 341.175 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The quantities µf and µbemf are representative of the same phenomenon – the expression of the Lorentz force"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "level" : "2",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.631, 306.022, 279.874, 317.052 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "– but have neither the same causes nor the same effects."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.621, 257.656, 282.016, 304.931 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Lorentz expresses that the charge carriers of a conductor immersed in a (non-collinear) magnetic field are subjected to a force when moving and this according to the following relation:"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 120.59, 233.91, 286.3, 253.896 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman-Italic",
    "font size" : 9.588,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Fq qEvB (7)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.636, 176.871, 286.145, 224.06 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "With Fq the force exerted on a charge carrier of electrical charge q subjected to an electric field E, undergoing a speed v, and immersed in a magnetic field B."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.636, 152.628, 286.124, 175.779 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The generalized expression of this force on a conductor of length l is:"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.83, 106.678, 286.305, 143.145 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "F  Bli fi (8) The current i flowing in the coil generates a force F"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.637, 94.352, 251.9, 105.596 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "on the latter with respect to the force constant µf."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 56.647, 58.436, 285.946, 93.589 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The magnitude µbemf expresses the force undergone by the electrons in a conductor which moves in a magnetic field. The displacement of the coil generates an"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.053, 747.449, 538.253, 770.481 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "electric potential at the conductor terminals with respect to the bemf constant."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.044, 650.817, 538.522, 746.338 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We study a system which on the one hand generates a force due to an applied current and on the other hand which creates an electrical potential with respect to the speed of the coil. This approach forms the basis of the development of a two-port network model and goes beyond the scope of this work. Different authors such as York, Smallwood or Guillaume develop this aspect deeper in detail [11–13]."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.053, 610.294, 538.321, 632.945 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "4.1 Experimental determination of the force constant"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.053, 541.815, 538.602, 601.241 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "First, we used the assembly already presented in Figure 3 which blocks the vibrating table. A voltage is applied to the terminals which generates a current, which creates a force. Due to the rigid structure, the table cannot move and no bemf can settle."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.053, 505.69, 538.412, 540.843 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "A measurement of the force with respect to the current yields the force constant. The latter is shown in Figure 9 for three different drive voltages."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.053, 421.08, 538.522, 504.599 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We immediately notice a very strong structural response at 660 Hz due to the resonance of the structure originally intended to be rigid. By performing a modal analysis of this structure, we obtained its frequency response. The synthesized curve has been superimposed on the measured curves to emphasize the response due to the resonance of the beam structure."
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.0, 267.34, 538.9, 412.19 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "00945e98ea0970dcffb8f548336df3a0137f_page_5_pg1_images/imageFile1.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "caption",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Caption",
    "id" : 24,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.05, 215.273, 538.622, 257.64 ],
    "linked content id" : 23,
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Fig. 9. Force Constant analysis for different drive voltages. The synthesized FRF of the beam structure is superimposed on the measured curves to emphasize the response due to the resonance of the beam structure."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 25,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.047, 170.512, 538.357, 205.695 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Beyond this, the measurements tend to show an increase in damping with the drive voltage underlying a possible non-linearity of the system."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 26,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 309.037, 61.801, 538.535, 169.421 ],
    "font" : "Arial-BoldMT",
    "font size" : 9.96,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "At this point any deduction is dangerous and should be avoided. Further analysis is required. Nevertheless, in the area below 100 Hz, the relative theoretical error due to the resonating structure with respect to the unity does not exceed 3%. Despite a relative variation in amplitude of 5% over the frequencies, the value of µf is considered to be constant. Its average value is 7.8493 N/A against an initial value form the manufacturer of 6.4 N/A. These data are shown in Table 3."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 29,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 297.264, 13.811, 302.264, 24.971 ],
    "font" : "Times-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "5"
  } ]
}